    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/saladin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saladin.jpg" alt="Saladin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Nasir_I_Salah_al-Din_Yusuf_(Saladin)._AH_564-589_(1169-1193_CE)_%C3%86_Dirham_(30.1mm,_13.28_g,_6h)._Without_mint-name._Dated_AH_586_(AD_1190-91)._Sultan_sitting_facing,_cross-legged,_on_high-backed_throne_(obverse).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group (CNG), CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ayyubid Empire was built by one man, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Salah al-Din Yusuf ibn Ayyub</a>, known in the West as Saladin. He was a Kurdish commander of exceptional political intelligence who began as a constrained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and became the master of the largest Muslim realm between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>. Saladin's path to power began in March 1169, when his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirkuh">Shirkuh</a>, the Zengid general who had taken control of Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, died suddenly and Saladin was appointed vizier in his place. Two years later, as the last Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-adid">al-Adid</a> lay dying in September 1171, Saladin had the Friday sermon pronounced in the name of the Abbasid caliph of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. This ended the Shi'a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> and returned Egypt to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> allegiance, and the Ayyubid state dates from this moment. At first Saladin was held back by his nominal subordination to the Zengid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, but Nur al-Din's death in May 1174 freed him to expand on his own account. He took Damascus that autumn and absorbed the Zengid principalities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> one after another, by military pressure and by diplomacy. He sent his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turanshah">Turanshah</a> to conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> in 1174, which secured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> route, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> in 1183 and received the submission of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> in 1186. The purpose of this long consolidation was explicit: Saladin was encircling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader states</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> before delivering the decisive blow. The cause he fought for was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a>, above all the recovery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> from Christian rule, and it gave his empire a purpose and a legitimacy that did not depend on the Kurdish origins of his family.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cairo-citadel"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cairo-citadel.jpg" alt="Cairo Citadel" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cairo-citadel">Cairo Citadel</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flickr_-_HuTect_ShOts_-_Citadel_of_Salah_El.Din_and_Masjid_Muhammad_Ali_%D9%82%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%A9_%D8%B5%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%AD_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AF%D9%8A%D9%86_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%8A%D9%88%D8%A8%D9%8A_%D9%88%D9%85%D8%B3%D8%AC%D8%AF_%D9%85%D8%AD%D9%85%D8%AF_%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%8A_-_Cairo_-_Egypt_-_17_04_2010_(4).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ahmed Al.Badawy from Cairo, Egypt, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ayyubid Empire governed the most economically productive region of the medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>. It held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, sustained by the annual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> flood, and the commercial centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, the Levantine coast and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Upper Mesopotamia</a>. Egypt's agricultural surplus came from the fertile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a> and the irrigation networks of the Nile valley. It financed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>'s campaigns and the court culture of the Ayyubid sultans, and was distributed to commanders through the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">iqta</a></em> system of revenue assignments. Egyptian commerce developed significantly in the period. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo-citadel">Cairo Citadel</a>, begun by Saladin in 1176 and expanded by his successors, was the seat of government and a sign of the dynasty's spending on the city. Among the sultans <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-kamil">al-Kamil</a> in particular promoted trade with the Italian maritime republics: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> secured commercial privileges in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> and brought European goods into Egypt in exchange for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>, alum, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and textiles. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Syria-Palestine</a> corridor was one of the busiest trading regions of the medieval world, connecting the overland routes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>. Ayyubid rulers tolerated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> enclaves on the coast, an arrangement formalised in successive truces from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-jaffa-1192">Treaty of Jaffa (1192)</a> onward. This was partly a commercial calculation, because the Frankish ports channelled Mediterranean trade on which Muslim merchants also depended. The empire's economic weakness was its division into semi-autonomous principalities. Revenues were split among competing princes, and the resources for sustained campaigns against either the Crusaders or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> could not be gathered.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin.jpg" alt="Battle of Hattin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saladin_Guy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Matthew Paris, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ayyubid army was built on the Kurdish and Turkic warrior traditions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>'s milieu. Its core was a professional cavalry of mamluks, slave soldiers mostly of Turkic origin, supplemented by Kurdish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> contingents, Arab auxiliaries and the forces of subordinate princes. Saladin's gift lay less in tactics than in strategy and psychology. He understood that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader states</a> could only be defeated by a unified Muslim army that denied them the chance to exploit divisions among their neighbours, and he consolidated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> patiently in order to create that force. The strategy reached its end at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a> on 4 July 1187. Saladin drew the Crusader army out from the springs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sepphoris">Sephoria</a> into waterless terrain at the height of summer and denied it access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-galilee">Sea of Galilee</a>, so that the entire field army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a> had to fight under extreme disadvantage. The victory was total: the army was destroyed, the relic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-cross">True Cross</a> captured and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan">King Guy</a> of Lusignan taken prisoner. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> surrendered on 2 October 1187. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a> (1189–1192) showed the limits of Ayyubid power against determined European opponents. Saladin lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> to the combined forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I of England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II of France</a> in 1191 and was defeated in the field at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsuf">Arsuf</a>, though he retained Jerusalem under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-jaffa-1192">Treaty of Jaffa</a>. Later Ayyubid armies performed worse. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultans</a> spent their resources suppressing the ambitions of rival Ayyubid princes more than confronting external enemies. They also relied more and more on mamluk regiments, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/as-salih-ayyub">al-Salih Ayyub</a>'s Bahriyya, and so created a professional military establishment that proved more durable and cohesive than the dynasty it served.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/damascus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/damascus.jpg" alt="Damascus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Damascus,_Syria,_Panoramic_view_of_Damascus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vyacheslav Argenberg, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> distributed the empire's territories as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanages</a> among his kinsmen, and Ayyubid government was pulled between that decision and the practical requirements of centralised rule. His reasoning was practical. By giving his brothers, sons and nephews direct control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Jazira</a>, he secured their loyalty and their military contribution to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> while retaining supremacy as sultan. The result was a decentralised family confederation, often described as semi-feudal, in which each prince maintained his own court, army and revenues while acknowledging the senior sultan's authority. Egypt, the economic heartland, was usually the senior ruler's direct domain and the principal source of the resources that sustained central authority. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo-citadel">Cairo Citadel</a> was built on a spur of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muqattam-hill">Muqattam Hills</a> overlooking the city and continuously developed by Saladin's successors. It became the administrative centre of Ayyubid Egypt and is among the most impressive monuments of medieval Islamic rule to survive. The Ayyubid sultans patronised religious scholarship, endowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> that consolidated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> learning in former Fatimid Egypt, and restored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dome-of-the-rock">Dome of the Rock</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-aqsa-mosque">al-Aqsa Mosque</a> to Muslim worship after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-jerusalem-1187">reconquest of Jerusalem</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shafii-school">Shafi'i</a> jurisprudence predominated at the Ayyubid court, though the dynasty also endowed institutions for the other Sunni schools. The sultans cultivated the religious legitimacy they needed as defenders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and protectors of the holy places. The critical failure was the absence of any accepted mechanism of succession. There was no rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primogeniture">primogeniture</a>, and the death of a senior sultan repeatedly set his heirs fighting, which drained the dynasty's military and financial resources.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jerusalem.jpg" alt="Jerusalem" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_Mount_(Aerial_view,_2007)_07.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Godot13, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ayyubid Empire is remembered chiefly for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-jerusalem-1187">reconquest of Jerusalem</a> in 1187 was followed by relatively merciful treatment of its Christian inhabitants, in sharp contrast to the massacre that accompanied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>'s capture of the city in 1099, and from this grew one of the medieval world's longest-lived legends of noble warfare. Saladin's reputation crossed religious boundaries. Christian writers celebrated him as a model of chivalric virtue even though he had taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> from them, and his exchanges of courtesies with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a> passed into Western literary tradition. The Ayyubid reconquest permanently changed the political geography of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> coastal states that survived the Third Crusade lasted another century but never again threatened Muslim dominance of the interior, and the balance of power established after Hattin set the course that led to the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> in 1291. The dynasty founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and this patronage strengthened the institutions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> learning in the region. The Ayyubid period also saw significant intellectual and artistic production, particularly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, where the Citadel was rebuilt in its present form. The dynasty's greatest unintended legacy was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> that replaced it. The slave-soldier system on which the later Ayyubids relied produced an institution of exceptional military effectiveness. It defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ain-jalut">Ain Jalut</a> in 1260, the first decisive check to Mongol expansion in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>, and governed Egypt and Syria until the Ottoman conquest of 1517.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ain-jalut"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-ain-jalut.jpg" alt="Battle of Ain Jalut" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ain-jalut">Battle of Ain Jalut</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Campaign_of_the_Battle_of_Ain_Jalut_1260.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MapMaster, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ayyubid Empire's decline began with the decisions <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> made during his rise. He settled the empire's territories as autonomous principalities on his sons, brothers and nephews, above all in the arrangements of 1186. This secured the loyalty needed to win at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Hattin</a> and recover <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, but it ensured that his death would start a dynastic struggle capable of undoing much of what he had spent nearly two decades building. The succession conflict that broke out soon after Saladin's death in March 1193 was the first of many. His sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-afdal">al-Afdal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-aziz">al-Aziz</a> fought over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and only the patient statesmanship of Saladin's brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-adil">al-Adil</a> allowed the realm to be reunited under his authority by 1200. Al-Adil's capable reign (1200–1218) and that of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-kamil">al-Kamil</a> (1218–1238) showed that a strong sultan could hold the Ayyubid lands together, but each succession brought renewed conflict. Al-Kamil ceded Jerusalem to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Holy Roman Emperor Frederick II</a> by diplomacy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-jaffa-1229">Treaty of Jaffa (1229)</a>. He gave up the city whose recovery had defined Saladin's reign in return for support against his own Ayyubid relatives. This was the ideological low point of the dynasty and showed how far the founding ideal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> had been subordinated to dynastic survival. Egypt was lost to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluks</a> in 1250, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turanshah">Turanshah</a> was murdered by his father's own Bahri mamluks, and with it went the economic foundation of the entire system. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-invasion-of-the-levant">Mongol invasion of the Levant</a> in 1259–1260, which followed the destruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1258, sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and ended the principalities of Aleppo and Damascus under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nasir-yusuf">al-Nasir Yusuf</a>. It extinguished most of what remained, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a> survived as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> into the fourteenth century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ain-jalut">Battle of Ain Jalut</a> was fought and won by the Mamluks, not the Ayyubids. Of the two products of the dynasty's military system, the Mamluk institution had proved the more durable.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Ayyubid Empire was short-lived. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a>, and the dynasty was then displaced by the very mamluk soldiers its later sultans had relied upon. The state rested on the ability of a single founder and on the generous distribution of power among his kinsmen, and those same kinsmen undid it once the founder was gone.</em></p>

